```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
```

In this example, a multiple regression model is fitted
using program quality ratings (`QUALITY`) as the regressand/outcome variable
and number of published articles attributed to the program faculty members (`NARTIC`),
percent of faculty members holding research grants (`PCTGRT`), and
percentage of program graduates who received support (`PCTSUPP`) as regressor/predictor variables
using a data set from 1982 ratings of 46 doctoral programs in psychology in the USA [@NationalResearchCouncil-1982].

```{r}
#| message = FALSE
library(package = betaLB)
```

```{r}
#| echo = FALSE
if (!exists(x = "nas1982")) {
  try(
    expr = utils::data(
      "nas1982",
      package = "betaLB"
    ),
    silent = TRUE
  )
}
df <- nas1982
```

```{r}
df <- betaLB::nas1982
```

## Fit the regression model using the `lm()` function.

```{r}
object <- stats::lm(formula = QUALITY ~ NARTIC + PCTGRT + PCTSUPP, data = df)
```

## Estimate the standardized regression slopes.

The effect size functions take the output of `BetaLB()`
and use its `type` and `control`.

```{r}
std <- BetaLB(object = object, type = "robust", alpha = 0.05)
```

## Semipartial correlations

```{r}
summary(object = SCorLB(object = std, alpha = 0.05))
```

## Squared semipartial correlations

The `SCorSqLB()` function squares the signed-semipartial interval as a set.
If the signed interval contains zero,
the lower limit of the squared interval is zero.

```{r}
SCorSqLB(object = std, level = 0.95)
```

## Squared partial correlations

```{r}
summary(object = PCorLB(object = std, alpha = 0.05))
```

## Improvement in R-squared

The improvement in R-squared of regressor $j$ is $sr^{2}_{j}$.
The `blocks` argument gives the improvement of a set of regressors.

```{r}
summary(
  object = DeltaRSqLB(
    object = std,
    alpha = 0.05,
    blocks = list(grants = c("PCTGRT", "PCTSUPP"))
  )
)
```

## Cohen's f-squared and local f-squared

```{r}
summary(object = FSqLB(object = std, alpha = 0.05))
```

## Extracting estimates and confidence intervals

```{r}
scor <- SCorLB(object = std, alpha = 0.05)
stats::coef(object = scor)
stats::confint(object = scor, level = 0.95)
```

